---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import MarkdownToHtml from '@/components/tools/data/MarkdownToHtml.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="markdown-to-html">
  <MarkdownToHtml client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert Markdown to HTML</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Type or paste Markdown on the left, or drop a <code>.md</code> file. The preview renders as you type.</li>
      <li>Choose the flavour. GitHub-flavoured Markdown adds tables, task lists, strikethrough and autolinks to CommonMark.</li>
      <li>Switch to the HTML tab to see the sanitised markup, with indentation if you want it.</li>
      <li>Turn on <strong>Full HTML document</strong> for a complete, styled page, not a fragment to paste into a template.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Markdown syntax reference</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-max border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class="border-b px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold">Markdown</th>
            <th class="border-b px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold">HTML</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code># Title</code></td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>&lt;h1&gt;Title&lt;/h1&gt;</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>**bold**</code></td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>&lt;strong&gt;bold&lt;/strong&gt;</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>_italic_</code></td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>&lt;em&gt;italic&lt;/em&gt;</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>~~struck~~</code></td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>&lt;del&gt;struck&lt;/del&gt;</code> (GFM)</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>[text](url)</code></td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>&lt;a href="url"&gt;text&lt;/a&gt;</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>![alt](src)</code></td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>&lt;img src="src" alt="alt"&gt;</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>`code`</code></td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>&lt;code&gt;code&lt;/code&gt;</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Triple backticks</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;…&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>&gt; quote</code></td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>&lt;blockquote&gt;</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>- item</code></td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>1. item</code></td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>&lt;ol&gt;&lt;li&gt;</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>- [x] done</code></td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Checked checkbox item (GFM)</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Pipe table</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>&lt;table&gt;</code> with alignment (GFM)</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>---</code></td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>&lt;hr&gt;</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What the sanitiser removes</h2>
    <p>
      Markdown allows raw HTML, so a converted document can contain anything the author wrote, including a
      script tag. Each result passes through an allowlist sanitiser before you see or download it. Elements
      outside the allowlist (<code>script</code>, <code>style</code>, <code>iframe</code>,
      <code>object</code>, <code>form</code> and others) are dropped, all <code>on*</code> attributes are
      removed, and URLs are checked so <code>javascript:</code> cannot stay in an <code>href</code>. Links
      that open in a new tab get <code>rel="noopener noreferrer"</code>. When the sanitiser removes
      something, the tool tells you how much.
    </p>

    <h2>Line breaks: the most requested option</h2>
    <p>
      In standard Markdown a single newline is whitespace, so two lines of prose join into one paragraph. To
      force a break, end a line with two spaces or a backslash. GitHub comments, Slack and most chat tools
      treat each newline as a <code>&lt;br&gt;</code>, so pasted text often renders differently than you
      expect. The <strong>Single newline = &lt;br&gt;</strong> toggle switches between the two behaviours.
    </p>

    <h2>Fragment or full document?</h2>
    <p>
      The default output is a fragment: headings, paragraphs and lists with no wrapper, ready for a
      template, an email builder or a CMS field. Switch on <strong>Full HTML document</strong> and you get a
      doctype, <code>&lt;meta charset&gt;</code> and viewport tags, a <code>&lt;title&gt;</code> you control,
      and an embedded stylesheet for line length, spacing, code blocks and tables, with a
      <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> dark variant. Save it as <code>.html</code> and it opens correctly
      from disk as a finished page.
    </p>

    <h2>Heading anchors</h2>
    <p>
      Each heading gets a slugified <code>id</code>: “Getting started” becomes
      <code>id="getting-started"</code>, so you can link to <code>#getting-started</code> from a table of
      contents or another page. Duplicate headings get numbers, so no two ids are the same.
    </p>

    <h2>Private and offline</h2>
    <p>
      The Markdown to HTML conversion uses marked in this tab, so drafts, release notes and internal
      documentation stay on your device. The page also works offline. For the other direction, use
      <a href="/html-to-markdown">HTML to Markdown</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
